  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Additional rounds of showers and storms through Friday.\nCooler and drier conditions for the weekend.\n\nA quasi-stationary boundary will linger around Wednesday and\nThursday before fully pushing through Friday. Chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms will arrive as upper level shortwaves\nride along the boundary over the next few days. \n\nLatest forecast guidance in addition to radar trends have\nlargely backed off on any aggressive convection this morning,\nwith only a spotty shower or two around. Mostly dry conditions\nare now expected during the day Wednesday before another round \nof isolated showers and thunderstorms arrives Wednesday evening\nand into the overnight hours. Once again, the coverage and\nevolution of these storms will be reliant on upstream\nMCS/thunderstorm cluster propagation across the Ohio Valley,\nadding a frustrating level of uncertainty to the forecast.\nOverall, however, forcing will be rather weak with limited\nelevated instability to work with given that the timing of these\nstorms will be largely after the diurnal heating cycle in the\nevening and into the overnight hours. Deep layer shear will be\nonly modest, around 35 kts or so, but that overall weak forcing\nwill likely limit the severe threat.\n\nAdditional rounds of upper level shortwaves look to ride along \nthe quasi-stationary boundary as it gradually sinks south\nThursday into Friday. North of the front, a drier and cooler\nairmass will filter in while isolated showers and thunderstorms\nwill linger along and south of the front. The cold front looks\nto finally push through late Friday, finally bringing a more \npronounced break in convective potential and more pleasant \nweather conditions for the weekend. Highs over the weekend will\nbe in the low to mid 80s. Dewpoints Saturday will be comfortably\nin the mid 50s to low 60s before creeping back up Sunday into\nthe mid to upper 60s. Another round of showers and thunderstorms\narrive late Sunday into Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There is potential for minor tidal flooding \nduring the evening high tides this week along the Atlantic \ncoast, Delaware Bay, and tidal Delaware River, especially on \nTuesday and Wednesday.\n\nAs we approach the New Moon Wednesday, astronomical tides for \nthe evening high tide cycles will increase to around a half foot\nbelow minor flood stage in many areas this week along the \nAtlantic coasts of New Jersey and Delware, Delaware Bay, and the\ntidal Delaware River. With tide departures running around a \nhalf foot above average, this will raise a threat of minor flood\nstage being reached for these areas.\n\nWe will continue to monitor the tide departures in the coming\ntotal water level forecasts. Coastal Flood Advisories remain in\neffect for Wednesday evening's high tide cycle. At least spotty\nminor tidal flooding should be anticipated around the times of \nthe evening high tides this week with the potential for \nadditional Advisories depending on the tidal departures. The \nhighest astronomical tides will occur Wednesday. The threat of \nminor tidal flooding may linger through Thursday or Friday as \nwell.\n\nTidal flooding is not currently forecast for the Eastern Shore\nof the Chesapeake Bay.",
